# No Sensitive Git Commits

Never commit sensitive or personal data to git in this project — not on main, not on feature branches, not even "temporarily".

## Never commit

- **Credentials & secrets**: passwords, `LI_AT` cookies, CSRF tokens, API keys, session tokens
- **Environment files**: `.env`, `.env.local`, or any file containing real credentials
- **Browser session data**: `.linkedin-session/` (contains LinkedIn login cookies)
- **Personal data exports**: `*.csv` (`connections.csv`, `sales-connections.csv`, etc.) and generated analytics pages (`connections-analytics.html`)
- **Runtime state with personal data**: `scrape-state.json`, `download-connections-state.json`, `remove-connections-state.json`, `remove-connections-results.json`

## Never hardcode in source

Do not put real emails, passwords, cookies, or tokens in committed `.js`, `.json`, `.md`, or config files. Use `.env` (gitignored) and `process.env` instead.

## Before any git commit

1. Run `git status` and `git diff` — inspect every staged and unstaged file
2. **Refuse to commit** if any sensitive file is staged; unstage it and warn the user
3. Scan diffs for accidental inline secrets (emails used as examples are fine in docs; real credentials are not)
4. Only commit when the user explicitly asks — and still apply these checks every time

## Safe to commit

- Source code, `package.json`, `package-lock.json`, `.env.example` (placeholders only)
- `search-plan.json`, `README.md`, `.gitignore`
- Documentation with placeholder values like `you@example.com`

## If sensitive data was committed

Do not push. Tell the user immediately. Removing secrets requires history rewrite or credential rotation — do not attempt force-push to main without explicit user approval.
